# Case Analysis: Brain-Computer Interfaces and Cognitive Privacy in the Age of AI

CYBV 329: Cyber Ethics
Author: Sabina Nabieva

---

## Context

Advancements in brain-computer interface (BCI) technology are redefining the boundaries between human cognition and digital systems. Co(g)nect, developed by NeuroSphere Inc., represents a major breakthrough in non-invasive BCI technology, enabling users to translate thoughts into digital commands and text with high accuracy.

While the technology offers significant benefits in accessibility, productivity, and human-computer interaction, it also introduces unprecedented risks related to privacy. Unlike traditional data, BCI systems have the potential to capture neural activity, raising fundamental concerns about cognitive liberty, surveillance, and the ownership of thought data.

---

## Scenario Overview

Co(g)nect rapidly gains adoption across multiple sectors, including healthcare, productivity tools, and consumer technology. However, concerns emerge when users report highly accurate targeted advertisements based on thoughts they claim were never expressed externally.

Simultaneously, the U.S. government files a motion to compel NeuroSphere to produce user data, including raw neural signals and processed thought-to-text outputs, as part of a criminal investigation. NeuroSphere challenges the request, arguing that such data is highly sensitive and protected under constitutional privacy rights.

The situation escalates through congressional hearings, legal disputes, public debate, and national security concerns, raising questions about whether existing legal frameworks are sufficient to regulate BCI technologies.



